  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nYesterday (Saturday), turned out to be much wetter than majority of \nthe guidance indicated, with several pockets over 2.0\" along the \nnorthern Coastal Bend and southern Victoria Crossroads. This same \narea will be most favorable for additional showers and storms \nthrough today, resulting in a marginal to slight risk of excessive\nrainfall. A 30-50% chance for showers and storms this morning \nover the Coastal Plains will increase to a 50-90% this afternoon \nas a cold front pushes through. FROPA is expected by this evening,\nlatest CAM guidance trending faster, causing only lingering low \nrain chances to continue tonight. Majority of the mid-level lift \nsupport will remain to our northeast, limiting the southern extent\nof the severe threat. Instability will likely be limited with \nCAPE under 1500 J/kg, but with effective shear over 40 knots \naligned parallel to the frontal boundary, a line of strong storms \ncan't be ruled out ahead of and along the front as it pushes \nthrough. Damaging winds and large hail are the primary hazards.\n\nThe shallow cold air mass will allow for low overcast clouds to \nlinger through Monday, likely cooler than what the NBM has currently \nwith temperatures varying little night to day with the breezy \noffshore flow. Therefore, went with a blend of short-range consensus \nand the REFS to get max temperatures on Monday only in the mid to \nupper 50s areawide; still about 10 degrees warmer than the record \nlow maxes but it'll feel nice! Rain-free conditions with partly to \nmostly cloudy skies will persist through the middle of the week \nbefore moisture advection returns, bringing low rain chances back in \nthe latter half of the week. A shortwave next weekend will result in \na slight increase in our rain chances. \n\nMinor coastal flooding is becoming increasingly likely tonight into \nearly Monday morning as strong northeasterly winds enhance the Ekman \ntransport mechanism along with sufficient wave run-up from 8-9 \nsecond swell periods. There is a high risk of rip currents tonight \nthrough Monday, leading to especially dangerous swimming conditions. \nMinor coastal flooding may continue to be a concern into the middle \nof next week during high tide at night.",
